---
id: org.dxos.review.echo
name: Echo
version: 0.1.0
---

ECHO object-graph conventions a linter cannot express. Each `rule` block below
is discovered by the agentic-review harness.

## Extensions

| Term   | URI                   |
| ------ | --------------------- |
| `rule` | org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0 |

## Rules

```mdl
rule inline-obj-parent: Set parent at make time with [Obj.Parent]
  When the parent is already known at construction, pass it inline as
  `[Obj.Parent]: parent` on `Obj.make` (or a type factory that forwards to it)
  rather than `Obj.make(...)` followed by `Obj.setParent(obj, parent)`. Inline
  parent is the supported make-time API and keeps the cascade edge on the same
  statement as the object. Flag a newly created object that is then immediately
  parented with `Obj.setParent` when that parent was in scope at `make`. Do not
  flag `Obj.setParent` used to reparent or unparent an *existing* object, to
  set parent to `undefined`, or when the parent is only determined after
  construction (conditional, async lookup). Do not flag comments or docs that
  mention `Obj.setParent`.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/*.tsx
  grep: Obj\.setParent
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule obj-update-push: Append inside Obj.update with push, not spread
  Inside `Obj.update`, append to an object array with `.push` (or `.unshift` /
  `.splice` for insert-at-index). Do not assign a spread copy
  (`arr = [...arr, item]`, `arr = [...arr, ...items]`, `arr = arr.concat(...)`)
  — Automerge array spread is O(n) per append; `push` is O(1). Canonical
  example: `Obj.update(set, (set) => { set.tasks.push(Ref.make(task)); })`.
  Flag a spread/concat assign of an object's own array field inside an
  `Obj.update` callback (or an equivalent in-place mutation block on a live
  proxy). Do not flag replacing the array with a *different* sequence
  (`filter`, `map`, reorder, slice-out) — that is not an append. Do not flag
  spreads of local non-proxy arrays, or spreads outside `Obj.update`.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/*.tsx
  grep: Obj\.update
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule schema-field-uses-platform-reference-mechanism: A field naming another entity uses Ref, a relation, foreignKeys, or an annotation — never a duplicate ad hoc field
  This covers schema-field design; `operations-take-refs-not-ids` already
  covers the narrower case of an operation's own input fields. When a type's
  field represents an existing domain entity, an external system's id, or a
  well-known piece of schema metadata, it should use the platform's own
  mechanism for it instead of inlining a duplicate ad hoc field or overloading
  a generic key/value slot — the duplicate drifts from the canonical field the
  moment either one is edited alone. Flag a new type field duplicating fields
  already on a canonical shared type instead of referencing it; a custom
  external-id field instead of `meta.foreignKeys`; a well-known metadata key
  stuffed into a generic `meta.key` slot instead of its own annotation; a
  low-level DXN passed around instead of a typed `Ref` to the owning object.
  Do not flag a field with genuinely no existing canonical counterpart yet, or
  an id crossing a real serialization boundary where a lightweight string is
  the established wire shape.
  Source: 8 review comments across 6 PRs, e.g. https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/11018#discussion_r3103390875.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*.ts
    - packages/**/*.tsx
  unit: file
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule query-capability-extends-filter-query-dsl: A new querying/ordering/reference capability extends Filter/Query, not a parallel ad hoc mechanism
  This specializes `one-mechanism-per-concern` with ECHO's own Filter/Query
  DSL boundary, which a repo-wide architecture rule can't know without ECHO
  domain context: when ECHO needs a new capability for querying, ordering, or
  reference conversion, it belongs on the existing `Filter`/`Query` API and
  its typed helpers, not as a standalone type, a bespoke scope-specific
  option, or hand-rolled sort/parsing logic living beside it. Flag a new
  standalone type or option living beside `Filter`/`Query` instead of folded
  into it; a local reimplementation of sort/ordering or reference-conversion
  logic that a shared `Order`/DXN helper already provides. Do not flag a
  genuinely new query primitive with no existing analog to extend. Fix: fold
  the capability into `Filter`/`Query`, and call the existing typed helper
  instead of hand-rolling it.
  Source: 4 review comments across 3 PRs, e.g. https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/12615#discussion_r3790898660.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/core/echo/**/*.ts
    - packages/sdk/**/*.ts
  unit: file
  context: similar
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule schema-persists-source-not-derived-duplicate: Persist the canonical source only; derive the rest, and keep one identifier field per referenceable thing
  This covers persisted-schema duplication; mirroring an ECHO query's live
  results into a separate UI signal instead of subscribing directly is
  already covered by `write-through-the-live-object` ("derive, don't sync").
  When a derived representation — an AST parsed from a grammar string, for
  instance — is always computable from a source the schema already stores,
  persisting both independently gives the two a chance to disagree, and two
  fields that both claim to identify the same thing leave no single answer to
  "what is this thing's id". Flag a schema/type persisting both a canonical
  field and its independently-derived form; two fields on the same type that
  both claim to identify the same referenceable thing. Do not flag a derived
  value cached behind a memoized getter that recomputes from the single
  source when it changes. Fix: make the source the only persisted field and
  compute the derived form from it; keep one designated id field.
  Source: 3 review comments across 3 PRs, e.g. https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/9897#discussion_r2411539688.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*.ts
  unit: file
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

```mdl
rule parent-ref-backs-parent-pointer: Setting a child's parent pointer must be backed by an actual ref or annotation on the parent
  This is adjacent to `inline-obj-parent`, which covers *when* to set the
  parent pointer at make time; this covers the parent side needing a matching
  reference. Setting an object's parent pointer with no corresponding
  reference (or annotation) added on the parent leaves a dangling one-way
  edge — the child claims a parent that holds nothing pointing back at it, so
  any traversal or cascade that walks from the parent misses the child
  entirely. Flag code that sets a child's parent pointer with no
  corresponding ref/annotation added on the parent side in the same change.
  Do not flag a purely computed/derived parent-child edge that the codebase
  has deliberately chosen not to back with a stored ref, stated as such. Fix:
  add the ref or annotation on the parent alongside the parent-edge write, or
  enforce it as an invariant in the setter.
  Source: 2 review comments across 1 PR, e.g. https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/12675#discussion_r3816670221.
  scope: repo
  files:
    - packages/**/*.ts
  grep: Obj\.setParent|Obj\.Parent
  unit: file
  context: diff
  system-one: on
  severity: warn
```

## Appendix

```mdl
ext rule
  uri: org.dxos.mdl.rule@1.0
  desc: A machine-checked review rule applied by an LLM subagent or the System One checker.
  inline-prose: true
  fields:
    files:       Glob | Glob[]     # paths this rule reviews, relative to `scope`
    grep?:       Prose             # optional regex pre-filter over file contents
    severity?:   warn | error      # defaults to warn
    scope?:      dir | repo        # glob base: this file's dir (default) or repo root
    unit?:       file | pr         # one verdict per file (default) or per change set
    context?:    ContextKind[]     # diff | imports | importers | siblings | package | public-api | similar | test | pr
    system-one?: on | off          # off when no fetcher can supply what the rule needs
    question?:   Prose             # literal yes/no question for the System One checker
```
